# One JDK for everyone, from the build: nothing depends on the machine's java.
# Bytecode is Java 21; tests RUN on JDK 25, the JDK the gates have always run on.
# The two differ observably: BigDecimal.valueOf(NaN)'s message, which the PCT
# expected-failure pins record (Test_LegendLite_EssentialFunctions_PCT).
build --java_language_version=21
build --java_runtime_version=remotejdk_25
build --tool_java_language_version=21
# Tools run on the same JDK as the tests: java_run (//tools/java_run) runs the
# generators' TARGET-configuration jars — compiled against JDK 25's class library —
# on this runtime, so it must be one they were compiled for.
build --tool_java_runtime_version=remotejdk_25
# the engine's test clock (legend-engine minted its goldens in GMT) — one clock, everywhere.
# Not on Windows: there DuckDB, and any JVM not started through junit_test (which sets
# -Duser.timezone=GMT itself: tools/junit/defs.bzl), read the system's zone and ignore TZ, so a
# test that compares zoned text sets its session's zone itself (WarehouseJdbcTest, 2026-10-02).
test --test_env=TZ=GMT

# Windows: tests read the upstream trees and sibling modules through RUNFILES by
# relative path, so runfiles must exist as a directory (Windows defaults to a
# manifest only), and Bazel must create real symlinks (CI runs as admin).
common --enable_platform_specific_config
# WINDOWS PATH LENGTH: Bazel's default output root (C:\Users\<you>\_bazel_<you>\...) plus a test's runfiles
# plus rules_python's repository names put pyarrow's DLLs past what Windows loads ("The filename or extension
# is too long", main's CI run 37209395985: 252 characters). A short root on every Windows host, CI and desk
# alike (Bazel workplan P1-08): the longest such path drops to about 226.
startup:windows --output_user_root=C:/bzl
# Linux's C toolchain is the hermetic LLVM (MODULE.bazel, Bazel workplan P1-09), registered here because
# the repository exists with these targets only on a Linux or macOS host. A platform it does not match
# fails, never falling back to the host's gcc. `common`, so query and build see one configuration.
common:linux --extra_toolchains=@llvm_toolchain//:cc-toolchain-x86_64-linux,@llvm_toolchain//:cc-toolchain-aarch64-linux
common:linux --repo_env=BAZEL_DO_NOT_DETECT_CPP_TOOLCHAIN=1
startup --windows_enable_symlinks
build:windows --enable_runfiles
# Windows: bash, for what still needs one -- rules_jvm_external's Maven fetch (it fails
# without BAZEL_SH) and rules_js's launchers. Bazel looks for one in BAZEL_SH,
# then MSYS2's registry key, then PATH: on a desk with Git for Windows and no MSYS2, from a
# PowerShell or cmd prompt, it found none and every Maven repository failed to fetch
# (2026-10-02). So it is named: Git for Windows' own bash, at Git's default install path.
# Git installed elsewhere, or MSYS2's bash wanted? These lines win over Bazel's own search,
# so repeat both in your ~/.bazelrc with that path; the later line wins. Without that, the
# first shell action fails naming the missing bash.exe (review of #14, 2026-10-03).
common:windows --repo_env="BAZEL_SH=C:/Program Files/Git/usr/bin/bash.exe"
build:windows --shell_executable="C:/Program Files/Git/usr/bin/bash.exe"

# CI: `--config=ci` — every test's log on failure, and no progress-bar noise.
common:ci --color=no --curses=no --show_progress_rate_limit=30
test:ci --test_output=errors --test_summary=terse
# CI runs every test, every time (L1e, 2026-10-07): with a remote cache Bazel would otherwise answer an unchanged test
# with its last result, so a push would get no verdict of its own and a flaky test that passed once would stay passed.
# The cache's gain is the build: the native image, the planner, the bundles.
test:ci --nocache_test_results
# CI never updates a lock file: MODULE.bazel.lock must already match MODULE.bazel (and each jar
# pool's maven_*_install.json must match its artifacts: fail_if_repin_required in MODULE.bazel).
# A dependency edit without its lock fails here instead of resolving silently.
common:ci --lockfile_mode=error

# THE REMOTE CACHE (the build rebuild's L1e, 2026-10-07; OD-5): BuildBuddy, written by CI, read by any desk that asks,
# so a lane downloads what an earlier job built (the native image, the WebAssembly planner, the bundles) instead of
# building it again on its own runner. `--config=remote` and the key, `--remote_header=x-buildbuddy-api-key=...`, go in
# ~/.bazelrc: CI writes them from the repository secret BUILDBUDDY_API_KEY (gates-run.yml); a desk puts its own key
# there, never in this file. Only CI writes to the cache: this config uploads nothing (noremote_upload_local_results),
# CI's own rc turns uploads on; so what the cache holds was built by CI, whatever key a desk has. The event upload
# gives every build a page with per-action timings (bes_results_url). Measured 2026-10-07: an unreachable cache is a
# warning (Bazel builds locally, exit 0), but an unreachable event service fails the build (exit 38) under the default
# upload mode, so the upload is fully asynchronous here: it can never turn a build red. zstd compression on the wire.
# The timeout: 600 s, BuildBuddy's own quickstart value and ten times Bazel's default, for the largest blobs (DuckDB's
# library at 108 MB, the image at 22 MB) on a slow runner link; a stalled call cannot hold an action for an hour.
common:remote --remote_cache=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io
common:remote --bes_backend=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io
common:remote --bes_results_url=https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/
common:remote --bes_upload_mode=fully_async
common:remote --remote_timeout=600
common:remote --remote_cache_compression
common:remote --noremote_upload_local_results
# REPINNING READS NO HOST MAVEN REPOSITORY (Bazel workplan P1-25b). `bazel run @<pool>//:pin` on a
# `resolver = "maven"` pool (maven_test, maven_upstream, maven_runner) otherwise resolves through
# ~/.m2/repository, as its first repository and its cache (rules_jvm_external 7.1's ResolverConfig sets the
# "unsafe shared cache" on unless RJE_UNSAFE_CACHE is 0), so a stale or hand-installed jar there could be
# what gets pinned. With it off the resolver starts in a fresh temporary home. The coursier pools never use
# a shared cache (use_unsafe_shared_cache defaults to False). ~/.m2/settings.xml and ~/.netrc are still read,
# for repository credentials only (RemoteRepositoryFactory): neither adds a repository or a mirror.
run --run_env=RJE_UNSAFE_CACHE=0
# HEAPS. Each test's heap is its own `junit_test(memory_mb = …)`, the live peak each run prints
# plus headroom, which is also its scheduling tag, so CI and the desk run the same command line
# (Bazel workplan P1-21, 2026-10-04; it replaced CI-only -Xmx8g/-Xmx4g here). The build actions' own heaps
# are java_run's memory_mb and //tools/teavm:compile's -Xmx2g, each measured (P1-22): they replaced
# --host_jvmopt=-Xmx3g, which every exec-configuration Java tool ran under.
# java_binary launchers a test starts (//core:server under //query-store:lite_test and //query:tests,
# //datacube:app_postgres under verify_app_test) are not junit_test JVMs: they take the JVM's default heap,
# a quarter of the machine's RAM, as they do on a desk.
# THE 7 GB macOS RUNNER (gates-run.yml's low-memory lanes): one test JVM at a time. The heaps are the
# targets' own memory_mb; the lanes that runner runs are sized to fit it (pct/BUILD.bazel, spec/BUILD.bazel).
build:ci-small --local_test_jobs=1

# LOCAL SCHEDULING (2026-09-27, measured on the 10-core / 32 GB desk — GATES.md entry of
# that date): Bazel's own estimate of a test's memory comes from `size` (an `enormous`
# test is assumed to need 800 MB) while our JVM lanes peak at 0.7-8.2 GB, so with one
# test per core it ran ten fat JVMs at once and every lane's time was 3-7x its own.
# The heavy lanes declare their MEASURED peak plus headroom (`resources:memory:<MB>`
# tags on the targets) and the machine declares its budget: 60% of RAM for tests
# leaves the Bazel server, the OS and whatever else runs on the desk the rest. Bazel
# then packs tests by memory; each test's own memory_mb is its tag (tools/junit/defs.bzl).
test --local_resources=memory=HOST_RAM*.6
# No Maven jar is rewritten to stamp its target's label into the manifest (docs/BUILD_REBUILD_DESIGN_2026_10_05.md,
# section 5c): a clean //:java runs 99 actions instead of 118, //:wasm 198 instead of 257. Strict-deps is still
# enforced. What changes: for a jar of a rules_jvm_external pool (the test pools maven_test, maven_upstream and
# maven_runner, and the tools' maven_tools and maven_teavm), its "add this dependency" hint names the jar's path, not
# its label. The product's jars (http_jar, tools/deps/jars.bzl) keep the label: Bazel's interface jar carries it.
build --@rules_jvm_external//settings:stamp_manifest=False
# A local disk cache: a fresh worktree or a `bazel clean` costs seconds, not core's
# compile. CI does not use it (since 2026-10-07 it caches the downloads only, gates-run.yml).
build --disk_cache=~/.cache/bazel-disk
# ...kept bounded (Bazel workplan P1-28): when Bazel is idle, entries unused for 60 days go, and the cache is
# trimmed to 50 GB, oldest first. A desk's cache otherwise only grows.
build --experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_size=50G
build --experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_age=60d

# Python binaries start from a bash stub, not `#!/usr/bin/env python3`: rules_python's default bootstrap
# runs the host's python3 before the hermetic interpreter (Bazel workplan P1-07). Windows uses its own
# .exe launcher, which starts the hermetic interpreter directly.
common --@rules_python//python/config_settings:bootstrap_impl=script

# Each Python binary and test sees its packages through a venv of its own (<name>.venv/.../site-packages), not one
# directory per wheel named after the wheel (rules_python++pip+pypi_312_<package>_<tags>_<hash>/site-packages): the
# layout Python itself expects, and paths short enough for Windows, whose DLL loader refuses a compiled module past
# about 250 characters -- the first Windows run of the DataCube browser test's Python engine (2026-10-09) failed so on
# psygnal's (anywidget's) module. rules_python's venvs (on Windows since its 2.1), on every platform alike.
common --@rules_python//python/config_settings:venvs_site_packages=yes

# rules_js depends on aspect_tools_telemetry, which reports ruleset usage from
# every build unless told not to. This repository's builds send nothing.
common --repo_env=ASPECT_TOOLS_TELEMETRY=-all
common --repo_env=DO_NOT_TRACK=1

# TESTONLY ON GENERATED FILES TOO (Bazel workplan P6-14, P1-25): Bazel refuses a non-test target that depends on a
# testonly rule, and with this also on a testonly rule's OUTPUT FILE (a filegroup over a test binary's deploy jar).
# With the test pools testonly as a whole (MODULE.bazel), nothing that ships can reach legend-engine, legend-pure,
# the engine runner's stack or the test tooling through the pools' jar targets (a BUILD file naming a per-jar download
# repository by its canonical @@ label is the one way around, and nothing does). One of Bazel 10's flags (below).
build --incompatible_check_testonly_for_output_files

# BAZEL 10 READINESS (Bazel workplan P6-14, G14): the incompatible flags Bazel 10 flips that pass here today. CI's
# product job analyzes //... with them (`bazel build --nobuild --config=bazel10 //...`); a flag joins when it passes.
build:bazel10 --incompatible_auto_exec_groups
build:bazel10 --incompatible_check_testonly_for_output_files
build:bazel10 --incompatible_config_setting_private_default_visibility
build:bazel10 --incompatible_no_implicit_file_export
build:bazel10 --incompatible_resolve_select_keys_eagerly
build:bazel10 --incompatible_disable_starlark_host_transitions
build:bazel10 --incompatible_check_external_repo_source_dir_package_boundary
build:bazel10 --incompatible_disable_non_executable_java_binary
